89: Use on the degree-two parameter cover the literal
90: pullback
91: \[
92:           C^{(2)}_{j+\tau}=C_\tau E_n^{-j},\quad j=0,1.
93:                                                         \tag{SV.6}
94: \]
95: This retains the original ordinary and cap endpoint
96: squares and gives \(E_n^{-1},E_n^{-2}\) at its two
97: successive collars. No new seam is selected for the
98: doubled return.
99: 
100: ## SV.2. The closed ordinary product and exact trace
101: 
102: The three native normal transitions are the ones of
103: K47/K62 with the full units (SV.2). In the second
104: vertex product \((u,\eta_n v,\kappa_n w)\), their
105: based double-loop normal matrices are
106: \[
107:  \begin{split}
108:  K_0&=\operatorname{diag}
109:        (e^{2iJ(1-n)\theta},e^{2inJ\theta}),\\
110:  K_1&=\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{2iJ\theta}),\\
111:  K_2&=\operatorname{diag}(e^{2iJ\theta},1).
112:  \end{split}                                          \tag{SV.7}
113: \]
114: For example their first coordinate factors before
115: reference division are \(\eta_n/\alpha_n\),
116: \(\kappa_n/g_n\), and \(\eta_n\), respectively.
117: Their second coordinate factors are
118: \(\kappa_n\alpha_n\), \(g_n\eta_n\), and
119: \(\kappa_n\). This proves (SV.7) from the actual
120: native coordinate arrows. The determinant is
121: \(e^{2iJ\theta}\) in every row.
122: 
123: Use the explicit quaternion contractions of DZ.12
124: with
125: \[
126:  t=J,\qquad
127:  l=\bigl(2J(1-n),0,2J\bigr),\qquad
128:                  Q\mapsto H_t QH_{l_i-t}.            \tag{SV.8}
129: \]
130: The equality of the determinant loops makes the
131: first vertex product common to all three stars.
132: Each remaining determinant-one normal loop is
133: contracted by that displayed \(H\) map. The actual
134: normal-rescaling and cutoff-flow construction of
135: DZ.2 carries the higher derivatives and retains
136: the full nonzero unit germs. The scalar factors
137: with nonzero winding are the exact powers in
138: (SV.7); every other based unit has its fixed
139: logarithm. Thus this is a closed ordinary product
140: of the doubled marked plumbing. It is not claimed
141: to intertwine the physical circle.
142: 
143: The physical tangent and normal weights in these
144: characters remain
145: \[
146:                   w=(0,1,-1),\qquad d=(-2,-1,-1).
147: \]
148: Use the literal orbit map DZ.16 and its derivative
149: in the covering group of stable \(GL^+\). Their
150: degrees are
151: \[
152:  \begin{split}
153:  I_i&=t w_i+d_i(l_i-t),&
154:  I&=(-2J+4nJ,\,2J,\,-2J),\\
155:  W_i&=t w_i-d_i(l_i-t),&
156:  W&=(2J-4nJ,\,0,\,0).
157:  \end{split}                                          \tag{SV.9}
158: \]
159: The same generic vertex paths and rank-one endpoint
160: caps as in DZ.3 close these exact traces. The
161: parameter-dependent vertices are (SV.5), so they
162: commute with the diagonal physical action and
163: their endpoint traces remain independent of the
164: parameter. No extra vertex meridian is inserted.
165: The coordinate-frame changes extend over the
166: annulus times its normal four-disc, exactly as
167: in the proved stable-trace comparison.
168: 
169: The integral prism identities now give
170: \[
171:  \begin{array}{c|cc|cc}
172:  &V_{ii}&\delta_i&m_i^{\rm ord}&n_i^{\rm ord}\\ \hline
173:  C_1&2J&-2J+4nJ&-2nJ&2J(n-1)\\
174:  C_2&2J& 2J-4nJ&2J(n-1)&-2nJ .
175:  \end{array}                                          \tag{SV.10}
176: \]
177: Here \(2n_i^{\rm ord}=\delta_i-V_{ii}\) uses only
178: the actual local ambient-to-single-star frame map,
179: not the earlier scalar initial-variation assertion.
180: 
181: ## SV.3. The native pairs, exact flops and recurrence
182: 
183: The native \(C_1\) coordinates are
184: \[
185:  (c,a,b),\qquad
186:        (\eta_n v,g_nw,(g_n\eta_n)^{-1}u),
187: \]
188: and the native \(C_2\) coordinates are
189: \((a,b,c),(u,v,w)\). Their exact transitions are
190: \(z'=z^{-1},n'_i=zn_i\). On each star the change
191: to (SV.8) is
192: \[
193:                 q_H\mapsto
194:                    (UH_JU^{-1})q_HH_{l_i-J}.         \tag{SV.11}
195: \]
196: Its left degree is \(2J\), and its right degree
197: is zero. The inverse cylinder extension has
198: endpoint \(L^{-2J}\), with coefficient maps
199: extending over \(D^3\times D^4\). Thus the native
200: doubled pairs are obtained from (SV.10) by adding
201: \(2J\) to the left coordinate:
202: \[
203:  C_1:\ (2J(1-n),2J(n-1)),\qquad
204:  C_2:\ (2nJ,-2nJ).                                   \tag{SV.12}
205: \]
206: 
207: The boundary products and seams (SV.6) are literal
208: pullbacks of the single native products. Pull back
209: a connection with those exact boundary products.
210: Its return is the actual relative square. Any
211: connection used in (SV.8)--(SV.11) with the same
212: boundary product interpolates to that one relative
213: to the boundary. The free-coordinate map to
214: \(\mathbb Z^2\) therefore gives the actual single
215: pairs
216: \[
217:  C_1:\ (J(1-n),J(n-1)),\qquad
218:  C_2:\ (nJ,-nJ).                                     \tag{SV.13}
219: \]
220: This is injectivity of multiplication by two in
221: the free relative group, not division of a
222: seven-sphere class.
223: 
